  "body": "Found during the work on #2394 (example case turning/cone-thread).\n\n## What happens\n\nA part has a turned profile, a thread relief neck and a modelled thread (V grooves). The Threading operation needs the V grooves in its source model. The Profile Finish operation cannot finish the same model. Its verdict reads each place that the 0.8 mm nose cannot reach as rest, and the post is blocked.\n\nThree readings on the finished model of #2394 (cone 20 mm to 10 mm, neck 8 mm from Z60 to Z63, M10x1.5 grooves from Z63 to Z78, insert `TN-1600-0R8`):\n\n| Profile Finish window | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| Z20 to Z78 (cone, neck, thread) | `0.0119 mm gouge, 0.7028 mm rest` (the thread valleys) |\n| Z20 to Z60 (cone only, ends at the neck) | `0.0062 mm gouge, 0.2435 mm rest` (the nose stands over the step into the neck) |\n| Z21 to Z59 | no envelope warning, but 0.8 mm of the cone before the neck is not finished |\n\nA Profile Rough with `stock_to_leave` 0 on the same window gives the same `0.7028 mm rest`.\n\nThus no profile operation can cut the thread's major diameter (10 mm) on the model that the Threading operation needs.\n\n## What is expected\n\nOne model is sufficient for a turned part with a neck and a thread. The profile finish cuts the major diameter and the cone up to the neck. Material that a later groove or threading operation removes (the neck, the thread valleys) does not block the profile finish.\n\n## Workaround\n\nImport two models on the same axis: the turned blank (no neck, no grooves) and the finished part. The facing and profile operations use the blank as source. The neck groove, the thread and the part-off use the finished part. The case of #2394 does this.\n\nRefs #2394\n",
